/-!
# Algebraization of a completion quotient with torsion conormal

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0ALR, `lemma-quotient-by-idempotent`
(blueprint: lem-per-conormal-torsion-algebraization).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace AdicCompletion

/-- A quotient of the completion of a finite type algebra over a Noetherian ring is itself
algebraizable if a power of the ideal of definition annihilates its conormal module.
Here `RingHom.ker f.toRingHom` is the kernel `J`, `Ideal.Cotangent` is `J/J²`, and the
annihilation condition quantifies over every element of the extended ideal `I^c`.
The map is an `A`-algebra map for the induced `A`-algebra structure on the quotient.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0ALR, `lemma-quotient-by-idempotent`
(blueprint: lem-per-conormal-torsion-algebraization).

Proof:
1. Put $S=B^\wedge$ and $K=IB$. The finite-type Noetherian permanence theorem makes $B$
Noetherian; its completion $S$ is Noetherian and $IS$-adically complete. In particular the ideals
$J\subseteq S$, $K\subseteq B$ and $IS\subseteq S$ are finitely generated. These assertions use
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian], [lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-noetherian],
[lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal] and the definition [def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring].

2. The conormal hypothesis says $I^cJ\subseteq J^2$. For any $f\in IS$, also $f^cJ\subseteq J^2$:
the extended ideal satisfies $(IS)^c=I^cS$. Thus $J_f=J_f^2$ in $S_f$, since
$x/f^n=(f^cx)/f^{n+c}$ for $x\in J$; the reverse inclusion follows because $J_f$ is an ideal. The
finitely many generators of $J$ still generate $J_f$. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-fg-idempotent-ideal-idempotent-generator] to get $J_f=(a_f)$ with $a_f^2=a_f$, and
then to the localized surjection $S_f\to C_f$, whose kernel is $J_f$, to identify
$C_f=(S_f)_{1-a_f}$. The kernel assertion follows directly from fractions: an element maps to zero
precisely when multiplying its numerator by some power of $f$ lands in $J$. This also covers
$c=0$.

3. Apply [lem-per-punctured-idempotent-complement] to $S\to C$ and the finitely generated ideal
$IS$. It gives a quotient $S\twoheadrightarrow C^{\prime}$ for which
$\delta_f:S_f\xrightarrow{\sim}T_f$, $s\mapsto(s|_C,s|_{C^{\prime}})$, is an isomorphism for every
$f\in IS$, where $T=C\times C^{\prime}$. The $S$-module $T$ is generated by $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$
because both factors are quotients. Choose generators $f_1,\ldots,f_t$ of $K\subseteq B$. The map
$B\to S$ is flat by [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-completion-flat], and $B/K\to S/KS$ is an isomorphism
by [lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal].

4. Use the algebra datum with completed piece $T$, chart $B_{f_i}$, structural map
$\alpha_i=\delta_{f_i}^{-1}:T_{f_i}\to S_{f_i}=B_{f_i}\otimes_B S$, and the canonical chart swaps
$\alpha_{ij}$. The two compatibility conditions in [def-weil-pbc-formal-glueing-data] hold: on a
double localization both structural maps are the inverse of the same map $S_{f_if_j}\to
T_{f_if_j}$, and the swaps send $b/(f_i^af_j^b)$ to that same fraction with the denominators
reordered. Reordering three denominators gives the cocycle identity. Each chart is generated by
$1$ as a module, and $T$ is finite as just shown. Hence [lem-per-formal-glueing-finite-algebras]
gives a finite $B$-algebra $D$ and a compatible $S$-algebra isomorphism $D\otimes_B S\cong T$.

5. A finite set of $B$-module generators of $D$ also generates it as a $B$-algebra, so adjoining
these to finite $A$-algebra generators of $B$ makes $D$ finite type over $A$
[def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra]. The map $D\to T$ induces an isomorphism $D/ID\cong T/IT$:
explicitly, reduce $D\otimes_B S$ modulo $I$ and use $S/IS\cong B/IB$; the maps send $d\otimes\bar
b$ to $bd$ and $d$ to $d\otimes1$. Let $\bar e\in D/ID$ correspond to $(1,0)\in C/IC\times
C^{\prime}/IC^{\prime}$. The finite-algebra integrality theorem
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral] makes $D$ integral over $B$. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-lift-idempotent-upstairs], with base ring $B$, ideal $IB$ and integral algebra $D$.
It gives an etale map $B\to B^{\prime}$, an isomorphism $B/IB\cong B^{\prime}/IB^{\prime}$, and an
idempotent $e\in D^{\prime}=D\otimes_B B^{\prime}$ lifting $\bar e$ under that isomorphism.

6. The etaleness just obtained means that $\operatorname{Spec}B^{\prime}\to\operatorname{Spec}B$
is etale in [def-weil-etale-morphism], hence flat. Its affine ring map is flat by
[lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion], and it is finitely presented by
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-ring-map-iff-etale-affine]. In particular $B^{\prime}$ is a finite-type
$B$-algebra, hence finite type over $A$ by concatenating its generators with those of $B$. It is
Noetherian by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian]. The $B^{\prime}$-module $D^{\prime}$
is finite, generated by the images of the chosen $B$-module generators of $D$.

7. By [lem-weil-pbc-flat-iso-mod-ideal-powers], the canonical maps $B/I^nB\to
B^{\prime}/I^nB^{\prime}$ are isomorphisms for all $n\ge1$. They commute with the quotient maps
because all come from $B\to B^{\prime}$. Applying their inverses coordinatewise therefore
identifies $S^{\prime}=(B^{\prime})^\wedge$ with $S=B^\wedge$ as $B$-algebras. Let
$\beta:B^{\prime}\to S$ be the completion map followed by this identification. For finite
$D^{\prime}$, [lem-weil-pbc-completion-finite-module-tensor] identifies its completion with
$S^{\prime}\otimes_{B^{\prime}}D^{\prime}$. This is a ring isomorphism, since its formula
$s\otimes d\mapsto s\,\iota(d)$ preserves products on pure tensors and hence on their finite sums.
The explicit mutually inverse tensor maps $s\otimes(d\otimes b^{\prime})\mapsto d\otimes
s\beta(b^{\prime})$ and $d\otimes s\mapsto s\otimes(d\otimes1)$ now give $(D^{\prime})^\wedge\cong
D\otimes_B S\cong T$. All maps are $A$-algebra maps, and their reductions modulo $I$ give the
original identification $D/ID\cong T/IT$.

8. The finite $S$-module $T$ is $I$-adically complete by
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian], since $S$ is Noetherian and
complete. Thus $(T,IT)$ is henselian by [lem-weil-pbc-complete-pair-henselian]. The image
$\epsilon$ of $e$ in $T$ under the completion isomorphism is idempotent and reduces to $(1,0)$
modulo $IT$, by the lifting property and the reduction compatibility in the preceding step. The
definition [def-weil-pbc-henselian-terminology], applied to the finite $T$-algebra $T$ itself,
says that an idempotent of $T/IT$ has at most one idempotent lift in $T$. Consequently
$\epsilon=(1,0)$ in $C\times C^{\prime}$.

9. Put $F=D^{\prime}/(1-e)$ and $G=D^{\prime}/(e)$. The map $D^{\prime}\to F\times G$ is an
isomorphism: an inverse sends $([x],[y])$ to $ex+(1-e)y$; this is well defined because $e(1-e)=0$,
and the two composites are identities using $e+(1-e)=1$. Both $F$ and $G$ are finite-type
$A$-algebras, since quotients preserve a finite set of algebra generators and $D^{\prime}$ is
finite over the finite-type $A$-algebra $B^{\prime}$. Modulo every $I^n$ this product
decomposition is $D^{\prime}/I^nD^{\prime}=F/I^nF\times G/I^nG$. Taking compatible sequences
coordinatewise yields $(D^{\prime})^\wedge\cong F^\wedge\times G^\wedge$, with $e$ selecting the
first factor. Under the previous isomorphism with $T=C\times C^{\prime}$, the same idempotent is
$(1,0)$. Restricting to its image therefore gives $F^\wedge\cong C$ as $A$-algebras, as required. -/
theorem exists_finiteType_algebraization_of_conormal_torsion
    {A : Type u} {B : Type v} {C : Type w}
    [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [CommRing C] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (I : Ideal A) [Algebra A B] [Algebra A C]
    (hB : (algebraMap A B).FiniteType)
    (f : AdicCompletion (I.map (algebraMap A B)) B →ₐ[A] C)
    (hf : Function.Surjective f)
    (hJ : ∃ c : ℕ,
      ∀ r ∈ (I ^ c).map (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion (I.map (algebraMap A B)) B)),
        ∀ x : (RingHom.ker f.toRingHom).Cotangent, r • x = 0) :
    ∃ (D : Type (max u v)) (_ : CommRing D) (_ : Algebra A D),
      (algebraMap A D).FiniteType ∧
        Nonempty (AdicCompletion (I.map (algebraMap A D)) D ≃ₐ[A] C) := by
  sorry

end AdicCompletion
